---
import AdvisorResourcePage from "../../../components/AdvisorResourcePage.astro";

// The v2.1 PDF is available at /downloads/signals-guide-for-advisors.pdf.
const SHOW_PDF_DOWNLOADS = true;
---

<AdvisorResourcePage
  title="Signals Guide: founder coaching conversations for advisors | DoGood Agency"
  description="A free guide for exit and wealth advisors: spot the personal signals before and after a sale, choose when to raise them, and introduce founder coaching with care."
  path="/advisor-center/client-tools/signals-guide"
  section="tools"
  tone="heart"
  name="Signals Guide"
  lede="How to spot the signals, what to say, and when not to suggest help."
  download={SHOW_PDF_DOWNLOADS ? { href: "/downloads/signals-guide-for-advisors.pdf", label: "Download the Signals Guide (PDF)" } : undefined}
  blocks={[
    {
      heading: "Why advisors asked for it",
      paras: ["Advisors told us the hardest part is not knowing that the personal side matters. It is knowing how to raise it without the client feeling judged, and when to leave it alone."],
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="advd-block advd-signals" id="how-to-raise-the-personal-side" aria-labelledby="signals-raise">
    <h2 id="signals-raise">How to raise the personal side</h2>
    <div class="advd-signal-parts">
      <section>
        <h3>How to spot the signals</h3>
        <p>Before the sale, listen for cold feet, repeated doubts about buyers, or difficulty picturing life without the company. After the sale, listen for a loss of structure or purpose, or old habits returning in a new venture.</p>
        <p>Use what the founder tells you to open a question. A signal is a reason to listen, not a diagnosis.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>When to raise it (and when not to)</h3>
        <p>Make the personal side part of exit planning from the beginning, alongside business and financial readiness. If that moment has passed, follow the founder's lead when they mention their own uncertainty.</p>
        <p>Ask permission. If they do not want to talk, respect that and leave the door open. When their needs call for medical or psychological care, point them toward a licensed professional.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>What to say</h3>
        <blockquote><p>“As we plan this exit, let's cover three legs: the business, the money, and you. The third is easier if we start now, not at the LOI.”</p></blockquote>
        <blockquote><p>“You've said this part doesn't feel as settled as the company side. I work with someone who only does that piece, and I'd like to introduce you, if you're willing.”</p></blockquote>
        <blockquote><p>“I've known you for years, and I think this would really help you.”</p></blockquote>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</AdvisorResourcePage>
